course: dictionary
lesson: 5510
title: The word le
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: In French, every noun is either masculine or feminine, and the word for "the" changes to match it. This is grammar, not a sign of who is a boy or a girl. «Le» is "the" before a masculine noun: «le chat» means "the cat".
explain: The other words for "the" are «la» for feminine nouns and «les» for more than one thing. So «le», «la» and «les» all mean "the", and you pick the one that fits the noun.
explain: Watch the accents. «Où est le chat ?» asks "where is the cat?", but «ou» without an accent means "or", as in «Thé ou café ?» (tea or coffee?). The context tells you which one is meant.
explain: You will also meet «le» in English through borrowed words, such as «le menu» on a café board, and in names such as Le Mans, a town in France.
fact: «Le» means "the" in French, and it is used before a masculine noun.
fact: «Le chat» means "the cat".
fact: «La» means "the" before a feminine noun, and «les» means "the" before more than one thing.
fact: Before a vowel sound, French writes l' instead, as in «l'hiver» (winter).
fact: «Le» is not an English word, but English borrows it in names and phrases such as «le menu».
exercise: In «le chat», what does «le» mean? | and | a | cat | *the
exercise: Which word fits here: ___ table (table is feminine)? | le | *la | les | ou
exercise: In «Où est le chat ?», which word means "where"? | le | les | ou | *où
test: Which word means "the" before more than one thing, as in «les livres» (the books)? | le | *les | la | où
test: «Le printemps» is which season? | *spring | summer | autumn | winter
exam: In «le rez-de-chaussée», what does «le» tell you about the noun? | That the noun is feminine | That the noun is plural | *That the noun is masculine | That the noun is an English word
